  "content": {
    "id": "p49-q36-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "36",
    "prompt": "Let $0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p49-q36-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $\\sin2\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\tan\\theta}{1+\\tan^2\\theta}$, where $\\tan\\theta$ is defined.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac{2\\tan\\theta}{1+\\tan^2\\theta}=2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta=\\sin2\\theta$, where $\\tan\\theta$ is defined.",
          "worked": "Where $\\tan\\theta$ is defined,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{2\\tan\\theta}{1+\\tan^2\\theta}&=\\frac{2\\tan\\theta}{\\sec^2\\theta}\\\\\\frac{2\\tan\\theta}{1+\\tan^2\\theta}&=2\\tan\\theta\\cos^2\\theta\\\\\\frac{2\\tan\\theta}{1+\\tan^2\\theta}&=2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\\\\\\frac{2\\tan\\theta}{1+\\tan^2\\theta}&=\\sin2\\theta.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p49-q36-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, solve $\\sin2\\theta=\\cot\\theta$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\theta=\\dfrac\\pi4,\\dfrac\\pi2,\\dfrac{3\\pi}4,\\dfrac{5\\pi}4,\\dfrac{3\\pi}2,\\dfrac{7\\pi}4$.\n\nUse part (a), then check $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$ separately in the original equation.",
          "worked": "$\\cot\\theta$ is undefined at $\\theta=0,\\pi,2\\pi$, so these values are excluded. For the remaining values where $\\tan\\theta$ is defined, use part (a):\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{2\\tan\\theta}{1+\\tan^2\\theta}&=\\frac1{\\tan\\theta}\\\\2\\tan^2\\theta&=1+\\tan^2\\theta\\\\\\tan^2\\theta&=1\\\\\\tan\\theta&=\\pm1\\\\\\theta&=\\frac\\pi4,\\frac{3\\pi}4,\\frac{5\\pi}4,\\frac{7\\pi}4.\\end{align*}$$\nAt $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$, $\\tan\\theta$ is undefined, but the original equation has $\\sin2\\theta=0$ and $\\cot\\theta=0$. Both are solutions.\nTherefore $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{4},\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{3\\pi}{4},\\frac{5\\pi}{4},\\frac{3\\pi}{2},\\frac{7\\pi}{4}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 118
      }
    ]
  },
